[Event "Strategy: the outpost, a knight nobody can chase"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/WoZlAQBV"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Spielmann - Duras, Breslau 1912 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "2k5/1p2rp2/1br2pq1/p3p3/P1P4p/1Q1P3P/4NPP1/1R3R1K w - - 2 27"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2k5/1p2rp2/1br2pq1/p3p3/P1P4p/1Q1P3P/4NPP1/1R3R1K_w_-_-_2_27"]

{ When nothing is hanging, chess is a fight for squares. Time to win some. You have White. An outpost is a square in his half, guarded by your pawn, that no pawn of his can ever step up to attack. Your knight wants d5, by way of c3. [%cal Ye2c3,Gc3d5][%csl Gd5] }
27. Nc3 { The knight is one jump from d5 now. It had no other road: his pawns cover every other square in front of it. [%cal Gc3d5][%csl Rd4,Rf4,Rg3] } (27. f4 { The f pawn goes forward to hit his e pawn, but your knight stays put and d5 stays empty. } 27... Rd7 28. Rf3 Bc5 { About level: your rook is stuck guarding the d pawn, and your knight never got to d5. [%cal Ge2c3,Gc3d5] }) (27. Rbd1 { The rook comes over to guard d3. Safe, but a rook minding a pawn does little else, and d5 stays empty. [%cal Gd1d3] } 27... Rd7 28. f4 Qf5 { Level: his queen and rook both hit your d pawn, so your rook must stay put, and the knight has not moved. [%cal Ge2c3,Gc3d5] }) (27. Qb5 { The queen goes after the a5 pawn, and your d3 pawn is left with no guard. [%csl Rd3] } 27... Qxd3 28. Ng1 Ree6 { Better for him: his queen ate your d3 pawn and chased your knight back to g1. [%cal Ge2c3,Gc3d5] }) (27. Ng3 { Heading for f5, the knight steps to g3, where his h pawn can take it. [%cal Rh4g3] } 27... hxg3 28. fxg3 { Lost for you: you got one pawn back for a whole knight. [%cal Ge2c3,Gc3d5] }) 27... Rd7 { His rook joins his queen against your d3 pawn. Breslau, 1912: Rudolf Spielmann, a master famous for his attacks, answered with a quiet knight jump to d5. [%cal Yc3d5,Rd7d3,Rg6d3] } 28. Nd5 { That is an outpost: no pawn of his can ever reach your knight on d5. It blocks his rook from your d pawn, and it hits his bishop, which your queen hits too. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc4d5,Rd7d5,Gd5b6,Gb3b6] } (28. Ne4 { The knight jumps to e4 instead. But that square is no outpost: his f pawn can step up to f5 and kick it. [%cal Rf6f5] } 28... f5 29. c5 { Chased, your knight needs a trick: the c pawn hits his bishop first. } 29... Bxc5 30. Nxc5 Rxc5 { About level, but a pawn down: your knight had to trade itself for his bishop, and your c pawn went too. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (28. Rfd1 { The rook steps across to guard d3, but it was the only guard of your f2 pawn. [%cal Gd1d3,Rb6f2] } 28... Bxf2 29. Nd5 { Level: your knight did reach d5, but only after his bishop grabbed the f2 pawn. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (28. Qb5 { Your queen hits the a5 pawn, but your d3 pawn is still hit twice, and nothing guards it. [%csl Rd3] } 28... Qxd3 29. Nd5 Bd8 { About level: the knight found d5 after all, one pawn poorer, because his queen took on d3 first. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (28. d4 { The d pawn steps away from the attack, right into the path of his bishop. [%cal Rb6d4] } 28... Bxd4 29. Nd5 { Level: your knight still lands on d5, but a pawn short, and his bishop sits proudly on d4. [%cal Gc3d5] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the outpost, the knight is attacked"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/SusMAvVI"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Gunsberg - Teichmann, Monte Carlo 1902 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "2r1k2r/pp3pp1/4pnp1/3pn3/3P1P2/1P2P2P/PB1R2P1/R5K1 w k - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r1k2r/pp3pp1/4pnp1/3pn3/3P1P2/1P2P2P/PB1R2P1/R5K1_w_k_-_0_18"]

{ You have Black. Your knight has just captured on e5, and two of his pawns can take it back. [%cal Rd4e5,Rf4e5] }
18. dxe5 { He takes back with the d pawn, and that pawn now hits your knight on f6. An outpost again: can you find your knight one? [%cal Re5f6] } 18... Ne4 { That is the outpost: your pawn guards e4, and no pawn of his can ever step up to attack it, because his d pawn is gone and his f pawn is already too far forward. The knight also hits his rook on d2. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4,Ge4d2] } (18... Nd7 { The knight steps back to d7. It is safe there, but it has no job, and a far better square was on offer. } 19. Rc1 Rxc1+ 20. Bxc1 { Level: a pair of rooks came off, and your knight sits on d7 doing nothing, while e4 stands empty. [%cal Gf6e4] }) (18... Ng8 { The knight goes all the way home to g8, the slowest road back into the game. } 19. Rc1 Rxc1+ 20. Bxc1 Ne7 { Level: after two trips your knight has only reached e7, and e4, where no pawn of his could touch it, is still free. [%cal Gf6e4] }) (18... Nh5 { The knight jumps to the edge at h5, where it has almost no squares. } 19. Kf2 f5 20. g4 { His g pawn attacks the knight, and every square it could jump to is covered. [%csl Rf6,Rg3,Rf4] } 20... Nxf4 21. exf4 { Much better for him: his g pawn trapped your knight on the edge, and it went for a single pawn. [%cal Gf6e4] }) (18... Ng4 { The knight jumps to g4, where his h pawn can simply take it. } 19. hxg4 { Lost for you: a whole knight gone for nothing, while e4 was waiting for it. [%cal Gf6e4] }) (18... Nh7 { The knight retreats to h7, on the edge of the board, where it can hardly move. } 19. Rc1 Rxc1+ 20. Bxc1 { Slightly better for him: your knight is stuck on the edge, far from e4, the square it should have taken. [%cal Gf6e4] }) (18... O-O { You castle, but your knight on f6 is still attacked, and castling does nothing to save it. [%cal Re5f6] } 19. exf6 gxf6 20. Bxf6 { Lost for you: his e pawn took the knight, and after your pawn took back, his bishop grabbed that pawn too. [%csl Ge4] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the outpost, queens and knights"]
[Result "1-0"]
