[Source "Tartakower - Seitz, Lodz 1938 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "3q4/pp4kp/1n1p2p1/Q2Pp3/4P3/1P3NP1/P4P2/6K1 b - - 2 29"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3q4/pp4kp/1n1p2p1/Q2Pp3/4P3/1P3NP1/P4P2/6K1_b_-_-_2_29"]

{ You have White: queen and knight each, and the centre pawns are locked. Your queen on a5 pins his knight on b6 against his queen. [%cal Ga5d8] }
29... Qb8 { His queen steps off the diagonal, and his knight on b6 is free again. Nothing is hanging and the centre is locked: what is your plan? } 30. Ng5 { Forward goes the knight, closer to his king. From g5 it eyes a square deep in his camp that no pawn of his can ever reach. } (30. Nh4 { The knight heads for f5. But that square is no outpost: his g pawn guards it. [%cal Rg6f5] } 30... Nd7 31. Nf3 h6 { Level: the knight went back to f3, and now his h pawn covers g5 too, the road you needed. [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e6] }) (30. Nd2 { The knight heads for c4. But that square is no outpost: his b pawn can still step up to b5 and attack it. [%cal Rb7b5] } 30... Nd7 31. Kg2 Nf6 32. f3 h5 33. Nc4 b5 { About level: the moment your knight reached c4, his b pawn attacked it. [%csl Ge6] }) (30. Qb5 { The queen steps up to b5, but the knight stays on f3, and he gets a free move. } 30... h6 31. Nd2 Qc8 { About level: he spent the free move on his h pawn, and now g5 is closed to your knight. [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e6] }) (30. Kg2 { The king takes a step, but the knight stays on f3, and his knight has time to run back. } 30... Nd7 31. Ng5 Nf8 { About level: your knight is one move late, and his knight already guards e6 from the back row. [%cal Gf3g5,Rf8e6] }) (30. a4 { The a pawn marches forward, on the side of the board far from his king. } 30... h6 { About level: while your pawn walked on the queen side, his h pawn shut g5, the door to e6. [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e6] }) (30. Nxe5 { The knight grabs the e5 pawn, but his d pawn guards it. } 30... dxe5 { Much better for him: his d pawn took your knight, and one pawn is all you got for it. [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e6] }) (30. Qxb6 { Your queen takes his knight, but his a pawn guards it. [%cal Ra7b6] } 30... axb6 { Lost for you: you gave your queen for his knight. [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e6] }) (30. Qxa7 { Your queen grabs the a7 pawn, but his queen guards it. [%cal Rb8a7] } 30... Qxa7 { Lost for you: his queen simply took yours, all for one pawn. [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e6] }) (30. Qc3 { Your queen stares at his e pawn, but his d pawn guards it, and your knight waits on f3. } 30... h6 31. Kg2 Nd7 { About level: his h pawn guards g5 now, so your knight has no safe road to e6, and his knight is back defending. [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e6] }) 30... Nd7 { His knight steps back to d7 to help defend. } 31. Ne6+ { Check from e6! That is an outpost: your d5 pawn guards it, and no pawn of his can ever step up to attack it, since his f pawn is gone and his d pawn has already moved on. His king has to move. [%csl Ge6][%cal Gd5e6,Ge6g7] } (31. Qb5 { The queen moves first, and the knight waits on g5. That gives him a free move. } 31... Nf6 32. Ne6+ { Slightly better for you: the knight gets to e6 a move late, and his knight is already hitting your e pawn. [%cal Gg5e6,Rf6e4] }) (31. Qc3 { Your queen moves to c3, but the knight waits on g5, where his h pawn can reach it. } 31... h6 32. Ne6+ { Slightly better for you: his h pawn kicked the knight first, and it reached e6 anyway, but you gave him a free move. [%cal Gg5e6] }) (31. Qa4 { The queen swings over to a4, and the knight is left waiting on g5. } 31... Nf6 32. Qc4 h6 33. Ne6+ { Slightly better for you: the knight lands on e6 after all, but two moves late, and only after his h pawn chased it. [%cal Gg5e6] }) (31. Kg2 { The king steps to g2. It is safe there, but the move gives his knight time. } 31... Nf8 { About level: his knight reached f8, where it guards e6, so your knight would only land there to be traded. [%cal Gg5e6,Rf8e6] }) (31. f4 { Your f pawn hits the e5 pawn, but meanwhile the knight on g5 stands still. } 31... Qc8 32. Qd2 Nf8 { Level: his queen and his knight both guard e6 now, so that square is closed to your knight. [%cal Rc8e6,Rf8e6] }) (31. Nxh7 { The knight grabs the h7 pawn, but his king guards it. } 31... Kxh7 { Much better for him: you gave a knight for a pawn, and your only attacker is gone. [%cal Gg5e6] }) (31. Qxa7 { Your queen snatches the a7 pawn, and his queen is guarding it. [%cal Rb8a7] } 31... Qxa7 { Lost for you: your queen went for one pawn, and the knight on g5 is on its own now. [%cal Gg5e6] }) (31. Nf3 { The knight goes back to f3, and its trip is wasted. } 31... h6 32. a4 Nf6 { Level: his h pawn now covers g5, so that road is closed, and his knight is settled on f6. [%cal Gg5e6] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, back to go forward"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/ii4xUHT2"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Lichess study Strategy course (Darko Polimac), ch. 1; Karpov - Spassky, Candidates match, Leningrad 1974"]
[FEN "r2r2k1/4qp2/2p3p1/p3p2p/PnQ1P2b/2N1B2P/1PPR2P1/5RK1 w - - 6 24"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2r2k1/4qp2/2p3p1/p3p2p/PnQ1P2b/2N1B2P/1PPR2P1/5RK1_w_-_-_6_24"]

{ Improve your worst piece: when nothing is happening, find the piece doing the least and give it a job. You have White. Nothing hangs, but your knight has no square forward: your pawns hold two, his c6 pawn guards the other two. Back to b1! [%csl Rb5,Rd5][%cal Yc3b1,Rc6b5,Rc6d5] }
24. Nb1 { Back to go forward. Anatoly Karpov, world champion a year later, found this retreat against the former champion Boris Spassky. From b1 the knight aims for d2, once your rook gets out of the way. } (24. Nd5 { The knight jumps forward to d5, but that is one of the two squares his c6 pawn guards. [%cal Rc6d5] } 24... cxd5 25. exd5 { Much better for him: his pawn took your knight, and one pawn back does not pay for it. [%csl Rd5] }) (24. Rfd1 { Doubling your rooks on the open column looks busy, but it gives the knight on c3 nothing new to do. } 24... Rxd2 25. Rxd2 Bg5 { About level: he swapped a pair of rooks, his bishop now eyes yours, and your knight is still stuck. [%cal Gc3b1,Rg5e3] }) (24. Re2 { The rook steps off d2 first, to clear the road: the plan, one move slower. } 24... Bg3 25. Nb1 Bf4 26. Bxf4 exf4 { Slightly better for you, but the lost move let his bishop reach f4 and swap itself for yours. [%csl Rf4] }) (24. Rxd8+ { A pair of rooks comes off first, with check, and the knight goes back after: the plan's two moves, in the other order. } 24... Rxd8 25. Nb1 Rd7 { Slightly better for you: his rook got to d7 first, and from there it watches the knight's road out. [%cal Rd7d2] }) (24. Ne2 { The knight backs up to e2 instead, the other way round, but from there it finds no target. } 24... Rxd2 25. Bxd2 Rd8 26. Bxb4 axb4 { Level: a pair of rooks came off, your bishop took his knight, and your knight on e2 still has nothing to hit. [%csl Re2] }) (24. Kh2 { The king steps to h2, a safe square, but it lets him swap rooks on d2 before your knight moves. } 24... Rxd2 25. Bxd2 Rd8 26. Nb1 Rd7 { Level: his rook owns the d column, and your bishop on d2 now blocks the square your knight wanted. [%csl Rd2] }) (24. Bc5 { The bishop chases his queen away, but it leaves e3, and the knight's road through d2 loses its guard. } 24... Qb7 25. Nb1 Bg5 { Slightly better for you, but now his bishop on g5 covers d2, the knight's way out. [%cal Rg5d2] }) 24... Qe8 { His queen steps back. Now clear the road: your rook on d2 stands where the knight wants to go, so trade it off on d8. [%cal Yd2d8] } 25. Rxd8 { A helper move: your rook leaves d2, and the knight's road is open. } (25. c3 { Nearly as good: the pawn kicks his knight first, and yours still reaches d2 later. The rook trade clears the road at once. } 25... Na6 26. g3 Rxd2 27. Nxd2 Be7 28. Nf3 { Better for you: the knight came round to f3 and hits the pawn on e5, which nothing guards. [%cal Gf3e5] }) (25. Kh2 { The king steps to safety first. Nearly as good, but the knight waits another move for its job. } 25... c5 26. Rxd8 Rxd8 27. Na3 Be7 { Better for you: the knight still found a road, through a3 towards b5, just a move later. [%cal Ga3b5] }) (25. Re2 { The rook steps aside to e2 instead of trading. The road is clear, but his rook keeps the d column. } 25... Rd7 { Better for you, and nearly the same: the knight can still come out, but his rook on d7 watches d2. [%cal Rd7d2] }) (25. Kh1 { The king tucks into the corner, a safe move, but your knight is still waiting on b1. } 25... c5 { Better for you, but he used the move to push his c pawn, and your rook on d2 still blocks the knight's road. [%cal Gd2d8] }) (25. b3 { A quiet pawn move that guards c4, while your rook on d2 still blocks the knight's road. [%cal Gb3c4] } 25... c5 26. Kh2 Rac8 { Better for you, but two quiet moves went by: his other rook came out to c8, and your knight has not left b1. [%cal Gd2d8] }) 25... Rxd8 { He takes back with his rook. Now the knight: out to d2, where your bishop guards it. [%cal Yb1d2,Ge3d2] } 26. Nd2 { Out comes the knight. Next it looks at b3, where it would hit his pawn on a5. } (26. Na3 { The knight takes the other road, through a3. Almost as good, but on this road it finds no pawn to hit. } 26... c5 27. Nb5 Na6 28. Nc3 { Better for you, but look: after three hops the knight is back on c3, and it has hit nothing on the way. [%csl Rc3] }) (26. Bb6 { The bishop jumps to b6 and hits his rook and his a pawn at once, while your knight stays at home. [%cal Gb6d8,Gb6a5] } 26... Ra8 { Better for you all the same, but his rook now guards a5, and your knight on b1 is still waiting for a job. [%cal Gb1d2,Ra8a5] }) (26. c3 { Kicking his knight with the pawn looks natural, but it lets the knight jump in to c2, where it hits your bishop. } 26... Nc2 27. Bd2 Rxd2 28. Nxd2 Ne3 { His knight lands on e3 and hits your queen and your rook at once. [%cal Re3c4,Re3f1] } 29. Qc5 Nxf1 30. Kxf1 { Level: after all the trades the material is even, and your edge is gone. }) (26. Kh2 { Your king heads for h2 first. Nearly as good, but the knight could come out right now. } 26... c5 27. Nd2 { Better for you: the knight still comes out to d2, one move late. [%cal Ge3d2] }) 26... c5 { He pushes his c pawn to free c6 for his knight, and now two of his pawns stand unguarded. Your knight to b3 hits both. [%cal Yd2b3][%csl Ra5,Rc5] } 27. Nb3 { A target at last: the knight hits both loose pawns, and he has no good way to guard them both. [%cal Gb3a5,Gb3c5] } (27. Kh2 { A safe square for your king first. About as good as the knight move, but the knight has a target right now. } 27... Be7 { Better for you, but he used the move to bring his bishop back to e7, where it guards c5, one of the two pawns your knight was after. [%cal Re7c5] }) (27. Nf3 { The knight heads for f3 instead, where it hits his pawn on e5. } 27... Bg3 { Better for you, a little less: his bishop hops to g3 and guards e5, and the two loose pawns were the easier catch. [%cal Rg3e5] }) (27. Bxc5 { The bishop grabs the c5 pawn, but it was the only guard of your knight. } 27... Rxd2 { Much better for him: his rook took the knight you left on d2, and you are a piece down for a pawn. [%csl Rd2] }) 27... Nc6 { His knight guards the pawn on a5, so take the other one: your knight takes on c5. [%cal Yb3c5] } 28. Nxc5 { At the start this knight had no square forward. Now it stands on c5 and you are a pawn up: you improved your worst piece. [%cal Gc3b1,Gb1d2,Gd2b3,Gb3c5] } (28. Nxa5 { Your knight takes the a5 pawn instead, the one his knight guards. } 28... Nxa5 { Much better for him: his knight took yours, and a pawn does not pay for a knight. [%csl Ra5] }) (28. Bxc5 { The bishop takes the pawn, and the knight that walked all this way to hit it stays idle on b3. } 28... Rc8 { Slightly better for you: the pawn is yours, but your knight is still idle, and his rook lines up on the c column behind his knight. [%csl Rb3][%cal Rc8c5] }) (28. Qxc5 { The queen takes the pawn, and your knight on b3 stays where it is, doing nothing. } 28... Be7 29. Qc4 Rc8 { Slightly better for you: the pawn is yours, but his bishop chased your queen back, and his rook now lurks behind his knight, facing her. [%cal Rc8c4] }) (28. c3 { A pawn move first, to keep his knight out of b4. Nearly as good, and c5 is still loose. } 28... Bg3 29. Nxc5 { Better for you: the knight collects the c5 pawn all the same, one move later. [%csl Gc5] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, the bishop behind its pawns"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/xxxWx6mq"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Levenfish - Alekhine, Russian championship 1912 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "r5rk/1p2b2q/p2p1p1n/3PpPpp/4Q3/2P1N2P/PP3P2/R1B1R1K1 w - - 7 26"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r5rk/1p2b2q/p2p1p1n/3PpPpp/4Q3/2P1N2P/PP3P2/R1B1R1K1_w_-_-_7_26"]

{ 1912, and you have Black. The centre is closed and nothing hangs. }
26. Kg2 { His king steps to g2, a mistake: pushing his a pawn to a4 was better. Your worst piece: find it and give it a job. } 26... Bd8 { Yes! On e7 the bishop had two squares, both backwards, because your own pawns block it. Through d8 it can get out. } (26... Nxf5 { Your knight grabs the f5 pawn at once, but his knight on e3 guards it as well as his queen. } 27. Qxf5 Qxf5 28. Nxf5 { Much better for him: you won one pawn and lost a knight, because f5 had a second guard, his knight. [%csl Rf5] }) (26... g4 { The g pawn charges forward, but your bishop on e7 stays behind its pawns and f5 keeps both guards. } 27. Rh1 { Slightly better for you, but nothing has changed for your worst piece: the bishop on e7 still has only backward squares. [%cal Ge7d8] }) (26... Rg7 { The rook steps up to g7, beside your pawns, and the bishop is still stuck on e7. } 27. a4 { Slightly better for you, but he got a free move on the queen side, and your worst piece has still not moved. [%cal Ge7d8] }) (26... a5 { The a pawn steps forward to a5, and the bishop on e7 waits another move behind its pawns. } 27. Rh1 { Slightly better for you, but his rook came to the h column while your bishop sat still, and f5 is as safe as before. [%cal Ge7d8] }) (26... Rae8 { The rook comes to e8, but the bishop stays buried, and he gets time for his a pawn. } 27. a4 Bd8 28. a5 { Slightly better for you, but a move late: his pawn on a5 now takes b6 away from your bishop. [%cal Ra5b6] }) (26... Bf8 { The bishop backs up to f8 instead, but there it meets the same wall of your own pawns. } 27. a4 Be7 { Level: the bishop went to f8 and straight back to e7, and he used the time to push his a pawn. [%cal Ge7d8] }) (26... Rac8 { The rook takes the half-open c column. It looks active, but it only runs into his pawn on c3. } 27. a4 { Slightly better for you, but your worst piece is still the bishop on e7, and now his a pawn is on the move. [%cal Ge7d8] }) (26... Rab8 { The rook moves to b8, behind your b pawn. It wakes up a corner piece, but the bishop is the one doing nothing. } 27. a4 { About level: your rook stands on b8 behind your own b pawn, and the bishop on e7 is still buried. [%cal Ge7d8] }) (26... Qxf5 { Your queen grabs the f5 pawn, but it was guarded twice, and now his knight can take her. } 27. Nxf5 Nxf5 28. Qxf5 { Lost for you: you gave your queen and a knight for his knight and one pawn. [%csl Rf5] }) 27. a4 { He pushes his a pawn. Your worst piece is free now: where does it do the most? } 27... Bb6 { Out in the open: from b6 the bishop hits his knight, one of the two guards of the pawn on f5. [%cal Gb6e3,Re3f5] } (27... a5 { The pawn to a5 first, so his pawn can never take b6 from your bishop. Slower, but it keeps the plan. } 28. Bd2 Bb6 { Better for you: the bishop reaches b6 all the same, a move later, and hits the knight on e3. [%cal Gb6e3] }) (27... Ba5 { The bishop goes to a5 instead, where it only hits the pawn on c3. } 28. b4 Bb6 { Better for you, but his b pawn chased the bishop on to b6 anyway, and a move is lost. [%cal Gb6e3] }) (27... Nxf5 { Grabbing f5 is still too early: the knight on e3 guards it along with his queen. } 28. Qxf5 Qxf5 29. Nxf5 { Much better for him: his knight was still guarding f5, and after the queens came off you are a knight down for a pawn. [%csl Rf5] }) (27... g4 { The g pawn charges to g4, but his h pawn can take it, and then f5 has one more guard. } 28. hxg4 { Level: you gave a pawn on g4, and his pawn there guards f5 too. You can still win his f pawn, but that only gets you even. [%cal Rg4f5] }) (27... Bc7 { The bishop to c7 instead, but on that diagonal it runs straight into your own d6 pawn. } 28. a5 { Slightly better for you, but from c7 the bishop still faces your own pawn, and his a pawn has shut the road through b6. [%csl Rb6] }) (27... Qxf5 { Your queen takes on f5, but the pawn still has two guards, and his knight can take her. } 28. Nxf5 Nxf5 29. Qxf5 { Lost for you: the queen and a knight went, and you got back only a knight and one pawn. [%csl Rf5] }) 28. Nc4 { His knight jumps to c4 and attacks your bishop. Where should it go? [%cal Rc4b6] } 28... Ba7 { The bishop steps back to a7 and keeps its diagonal, still aimed at e3. [%cal Ga7e3] } (28... Bc7 { The bishop steps back to c7. Just as good for now, but it gives up the diagonal, so his knight can hop back to e3. } 29. Ne3 Bb6 { Better for you, but you went in a circle: his knight came back to guard f5, and your bishop had to return to b6 to hit it again. [%cal Gb6e3,Re3f5] }) (28... Bc5 { The bishop steps up to c5, still on the diagonal, but there he can trade it off at once. } 29. Be3 Nxf5 30. Bxc5 dxc5 { Better for you: the pawn still fell, but your bishop was traded off, and his pawn on d5 is now passed. [%csl Rd5] }) (28... Nxf5 { Taking the pawn first leaves your bishop hanging on b6. } 29. Nxb6 { Slightly better for him: his knight took your bishop, so your worst piece paid for a single pawn. [%csl Rb6] }) (28... Qc7 { The queen comes over to guard the bishop. That saves it, but your queen no longer aims at f5. } 29. Be3 Bxe3 30. Nxe3 { Level: the bishops came off, his knight stands on e3 again, and f5 has both its guards back. [%cal Re3f5] }) (28... Bd8 { The bishop goes all the way back to d8, its first stop, and his knight can take the d6 pawn. } 29. Nxd6 Be7 30. Nxb7 Nxf5 { Better for you, but the retreat cost you two pawns, and winning f5 got only one back. [%csl Gf5] }) 29. Be3 { He puts his bishop on e3 to block the diagonal and offer a trade. What did his knight's trip cost him? } 29... Nxf5 { Your worst piece did the job: from b6 your bishop drove off his knight, one of the two guards of f5, and the pawn is yours. Alexander Alekhine, world champion from 1927, played your side of this position in 1912. [%csl Gf5][%cal Ga7e3] } (29... Bxe3 { Trading bishops looks tidy, but it hands his knight a way back. } 30. Nxe3 { Level: his knight is back on e3, guarding f5 again, and the chance is gone. [%cal Re3f5] }) (29... Bb8 { The bishop runs to b8, away from the trade, but it leaves the diagonal and lets his knight jump to b6. } 30. Nb6 Nxf5 31. Kh2 Ra7 { Better for you: f5 still fell, a move late, but your bishop gave up its diagonal and his knight got into b6. [%csl Rb6] }) (29... g4 { The g pawn charges, but his bishop can take your knight on h6 at once. } 30. Bxh6 gxh3+ 31. Kh1 Rg4 32. Qf3 Qxh6 { About level: the scramble won you a pawn, but your knight went for his bishop, and his pawn on f5 still stands. }) (29... Qxf5 { Your queen takes the pawn instead of your knight, and he can now trade off the bishops and the queens. } 30. Bxa7 Qxe4+ 31. Rxe4 Rxa7 { Level: taking with the queen let him swap off the bishops and the queens, and the extra pawn counts for little. [%csl Gh6] }) (29... b6 { The b pawn steps up to shield your bishop, but it leaves the d6 pawn to his knight. } 30. Nxd6 Rad8 31. Nc4 Nxf5 { About level: his knight took d6 first, so winning f5 afterwards only evened the count. [%csl Rd6] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, take away the guard"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/3ukeKYfs"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Schlechter - Nimzowitsch, San Sebastian 1912 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "r1r3k1/1p3ppp/p3pn2/1bB5/8/3B4/PPP2PPP/3R1RK1 w - - 0 20"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1r3k1/1p3ppp/p3pn2/1bB5/8/3B4/PPP2PPP/3R1RK1_w_-_-_0_20"]

{ San Sebastian, 1912: you have Black against Carl Schlechter, one of the best players of his day. The right trade: before you swap, ask which piece leaves the board. Take off the piece that guards his weakness, or his best piece. }
20. Bd4 { His bishop steps back. Now look at the pawn on c2: only his bishop on d3 guards it, and your rook already aims at it. Take off the guard! [%cal Yb5d3,Rd3c2,Gc8c2] } 20... Bxd3 { Your bishop takes the guard. He must take back at once, and right now nothing of his guards the pawn on c2. [%csl Rc2] } (20... Rxc2 { Grabbing the pawn at once looks tempting, but his bishop on d3 still guards it. } 21. Bxc2 Bxf1 22. Kxf1 { Lost for you: one pair of rooks came off, and your bishop went for a single pawn. The guard had to leave first. [%cal Gb5d3] }) (20... Bc4 { Offering the trade on c4 instead looks the same, but now he decides when to take. } 21. Bxc4 Rxc4 22. Bxf6 gxf6 23. Rd2 { Level: he took first, broke your pawns, and his rook now guards the pawn on c2. [%cal Gb5d3] }) (20... Nd5 { The knight jumps to the centre, but the guard on d3 stays where it is. } 21. c3 f6 22. f4 { Level: his pawn stepped up to c3, where his b pawn guards it, and your chance is gone. [%cal Gb5d3] }) 21. Rxd3 { He takes back with the rook, his mistake: the pawn on c2 is left alone. Taking with the c pawn kept it, at the cost of a lonely pawn on d3. [%cal Yc8c2] } 21... Rxc2 { There it goes: with its guard gone, his c pawn falls, and your rook lands on his second row. [%csl Gc2] } (21... Nd5 { The knight jumps to d5, but the free pawn gets one move to escape. } 22. c3 Rd8 23. f4 { Level: his pawn slipped to c3, guarded by his b pawn, and your extra pawn never came. [%cal Gc8c2] }) 22. Rb3 { His rook swings over and hits your pawn on b7. Keep your extra pawn: push it out of reach. [%cal Rb3b7,Yb7b5] } 22... b5 { The pawn steps to b5, where your a pawn guards it, and his rook bites on nothing. Now watch. [%tail][%cal Ga6b5] } (22... Rc7 { The rook drops back to guard b7, but it leaves his second row. [%cal Gc7b7] } 23. Bxf6 gxf6 24. Rd1 { About level: still a pawn up, but your rook is stuck guarding a pawn instead of raiding his second row. [%cal Gb7b5] }) (22... Rxb2 { The rook grabs a second pawn, on b2. But look who guards it. } 23. Bxb2 { Lost for you: his bishop was guarding that pawn, and it takes your rook. [%cal Gb7b5][%csl Rb2] }) 23. Bxf6 gxf6 { The right trade was the one on d3: it took off the only guard of c2, and the pawn fell. A pawn up, with your rook on his second row. [%csl Gc2] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, an uninvited guest"]
