[FEN "r1bqk2r/bp3pp1/p1np1n1p/4p3/4P3/PNN2PB1/1PPQ2PP/2KR1B1R b kq - 3 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/bp3pp1/p1np1n1p/4p3/4P3/PNN2PB1/1PPQ2PP/2KR1B1R_b_kq_-_3_13"]

{ You have White in this online game, and it is his move. }
13... Nd4 { His knight jumps into your camp, a mistake: bishop to e6 was calmer. The right trade again: which piece should leave the board? } 14. Nxd4 { Right: his best piece leaves the board. He has to take back on d4, and your queen is already looking at that square. [%cal Gd2d4] } (14. Nd5 { Your knight offers a trade on d5, but that swaps off his other knight, not the strong one. } 14... Nxd5 15. Nxd4 Bxd4 16. exd5 { About level: all four knights came off, and his bishop now stands on d4 where his knight stood. [%cal Gb3d4] }) (14. Kb1 { The king tucks away to b1: safe, but his knight on d4 is left in peace. } 14... Be6 { About level: he used the time to bring out his last bishop, and the knight on d4 still stands. [%cal Gb3d4] }) (14. Bc4 { The bishop comes out to c4, but his knight on d4 is left alone. } 14... b5 15. Bf1 { About level: his b pawn chased your bishop back home, and his knight still rules the centre. [%cal Gb3d4] }) (14. Bxe5 { The bishop grabs his e pawn, hoping the pawn on d6 cannot take back. } 14... dxe5 { Lost for you: his knight on d4 blocks your queen's column, so his pawn was free to take your bishop. [%cal Gb3d4,Rd2d4] }) (14. Bh4 { The bishop pins his knight on f6, but the knight that matters is the one on d4. [%cal Bh4d8] } 14... Be6 15. Nxd4 Bxd4 { About level: your pin cost a move, he brought out his last bishop, and when a pair of knights came off, his other bishop took back on d4. [%cal Gb3d4] }) (14. f4 { The pawn hits e5, the pawn that props up his knight, but it does nothing to the knight itself. } 14... Be6 { About level: he simply develops, and now his knight and his bishop both hit your knight on b3. [%cal Gb3d4] }) 14... exd4 { His pawn takes back, and now it attacks your knight on c3. Where should your knight go? [%cal Rd4c3] } 15. Ne2 { The knight steps out of the pawn's reach and hits it back: your queen and knight both attack d4, and his bishop alone guards it. Now watch. [%tail][%cal Ge2d4,Gd2d4] } (15. Nd5 { The knight hops to d5, a fine square, but from there it never touches the pawn on d4. } 15... Nxd5 16. exd5 O-O { Better for you still, but his knight traded itself off, and the pawn on d4 lives on. [%cal Gc3e2] }) (15. Qxd4 { The queen grabs the pawn at once, but his bishop on a7 guards it. } 15... Bxd4 16. Rxd4 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen, and a bishop and a pawn are all you got back. [%cal Gc3e2] }) (15. Nb1 { The knight runs home to b1, safe from the pawn, but from there it never attacks it. } 15... d5 { Better for you, but your knight went home instead of hitting d4, so winning that pawn now takes longer. [%cal Gc3e2] }) (15. Na2 { The knight hides on a2, safe from the pawn, but stuck on the edge of the board. } 15... Be6 { Slightly better for you: your knight sits on the rim, and his pawn on d4 has time to feel safe. [%cal Gc3e2] }) 15... d5 16. e5 Nd7 17. Nxd4 { That was the right trade: his knight left d4, and the pawn that replaced it has fallen. A pawn up, with your knight on d4 where his stood. [%csl Gd4] } 1-0


