[FEN "4Nb1k/7p/2q1b3/2p1ppP1/1pPnP3/3PB3/rP1R1Q1P/5RK1 b - - 0 29"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4Nb1k/7p/2q1b3/2p1ppP1/1pPnP3/3PB3/rP1R1Q1P/5RK1_b_-_-_0_29"]

{ You have White, in a master game from St Petersburg, 1914. }
29... Qxe8 { His queen takes back your knight, his only move. Material is level, and one capture looks free. Is it? Find the move that really wins something. } 30. Bxd4 { Your bishop takes on d4: that knight was his best piece, and the guard that mattered most. He has to take back. } (30. exf5 { Your pawn grabs f5 at once. But his knight on d4 still guards it. } 30... Nxf5 { Level: his knight took back on f5, guarded by his bishop, and now it hits your bishop. The guard should have left first. [%cal Ge3d4] }) (30. h3 { The pawn makes room for your king, but it gives his f pawn time to move first. } 30... f4 31. Bxd4 cxd4 { Better for him: his f pawn jumped to f4 and made your bishop take, and now that pawn walls off your queen and rook. [%cal Ge3d4][%csl Rf4] }) (30. Re1 { The rook slides over behind your e pawn, but his f pawn is quicker. } 30... f4 31. Bxd4 cxd4 { Better for him: once his pawn reached f4, your bishop had to swap itself off, and his pawns now cramp your pieces. [%cal Ge3d4] }) (30. Qh4 { The queen swings over to eye his king, but she leaves his f pawn free to advance. } 30... f4 31. Bxd4 cxd4 { Better for him: his pawn got to f4 first, the trade on d4 came anyway, and his pawn chain now reaches deep into your half. [%cal Ge3d4] }) 30... exd4 { He takes back with his e pawn. So, what did your trade win? } 31. exf5 { The right trade: the knight you took was the one guard that could safely take back on f5, so that pawn is yours. If his bishop takes it, your queen takes the bishop. [%cal Gf2f5] } (31. h4 { The h pawn steps up to guard g5: solid, but it gives the pawn on f5 a move to run. [%cal Gh4g5] } 31... f4 32. Qxf4 Bg7 { Level, and his f pawn falls this way too: it ran to f4 and was taken there instead. [%cal Ge4f5] }) (31. Qf3 { The queen piles up on f5 as well, but taking it straight away was simpler: the pawn can still step forward. [%cal Gf3f5] } 31... f4 32. Qxf4 Bg7 { Level: the pawn was taken on f4 instead, and his dark bishop came out to g7. [%cal Ge4f5] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the weak colour, the queen walks in"]
[Result "1-0"]
