[FEN "2brk3/1p2qpn1/2p1p1p1/2Np3p/3PP3/2P1QPB1/RP4PP/4K3 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2brk3/1p2qpn1/2p1p1p1/2Np3p/3PP3/2P1QPB1/RP4PP/4K3_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ You have White, and this is the weak colour. A pawn only guards squares of the colour it stands on: his pawns all stand on light squares and his dark bishop is gone, so no pawn or bishop of his guards a dark square. Queen to h6! [%cal Ye3h6] }
1. Qh6 { Your queen lands on h6, a dark square nothing of his attacks, and hits the knight on g7, which has no guard. His light bishop can only watch. [%cal Gh6g7] } (1. Qf4 { The queen goes to f4, a dark square too, but from there she does not touch his knight. } 1... g5 { Much better for you still, but his g pawn kicks your queen from g5, and now a pawn of his guards two dark squares. On h6 she would have hit the knight at once. [%cal Rg5f4,Ge3h6] }) (1. Ra8 { The rook runs to a8 and pins his bishop to his rook, but a rook sees light and dark squares alike. [%cal Ga8d8] } 1... g5 { Better for you still, but the rook move gave him time to push his g pawn onto g5, a dark square. Your queen should have jumped in first. [%cal Rg5f4,Ge3h6] }) (1. Be5 { The bishop jumps to e5 and hits the knight on g7, but his f pawn can block it and kick it at once. } 1... f6 2. Bg3 { About level: your bishop had to go back, and his f pawn on f6 now guards two dark squares. [%cal Rf6e5,Rf6g5,Ge3h6] }) (1. Bc7 { The bishop hits his rook from c7, but it lands on the row his queen guards. [%cal Re7c7] } 1... Qxc7 { Better for him: his queen took your bishop for nothing. [%cal Ge3h6] }) 1... f6 { His f pawn steps to f6, so his queen can guard the knight along the row. But that pawn was the only guard of his g pawn: take it, with check! [%cal Re7g7,Yh6g6] } 2. Qxg6+ { Check, and the g pawn is yours: once the f pawn moved, nothing guarded it. [%cal Gg6e8] } (2. Ra8 { The rook jumps to a8 and stares at his bishop: just as strong, because your rook joins the attack. } 2... b6 3. Qxg6+ Kf8 4. Nd3 { Much better for you: the g pawn still fell, one move later, but his b pawn had time to chase your knight from c5. [%cal Gh6g6] }) (2. e5 { The pawn hits his f pawn from e5, and it is just as strong, though his knight jumps out to f5 first and hits your queen. } 2... Nf5 3. Qxg6+ Qf7 4. Qxf6 Qxf6 5. exf6 { Much better for you too, two pawns up with the queens off: this road works just as well, it only lets his knight out first. [%cal Gh6g6] }) (2. Qxg7 { The queen grabs the knight, but his queen guards it now. [%cal Re7g7] } 2... Qxg7 { Lost for you: his queen took yours, and a knight is all you got for her. [%cal Gh6g6] }) 2... Qf7 { His queen blocks on f7 and offers a trade. Keep her! Back to h6, where she hits his knight again, and his f pawn too. [%cal Yg6h6] } 3. Qh6 { Back on h6, your queen presses his f pawn and his knight, both on dark squares and both guarded only by his queen. Now watch. [%tail][%cal Gh6f6,Gh6g7] } (3. Qxf7+ { Trading queens with check is tempting when you are ahead, but your best attacker leaves the board. } 3... Kxf7 { Much better for you, a pawn up, but with the queens gone his king on f7 guards the dark squares around it himself. [%cal Gg6h6] }) (3. Qh7 { The queen slides to h7, a light square: just as good for now, but it lets go of his f pawn. } 3... Ke7 { Much better for you, as good as going back to h6, but his king steps up to guard the f pawn, and your queen no longer presses it. [%cal Gg6h6] }) (3. Qxf6 { The queen grabs the f pawn, but his queen stands right next to it. } 3... Qxf6 { Lost for you: his queen took yours for one pawn. [%cal Rf7f6,Gg6h6] }) (3. Qxg7 { Taking the knight looks free, but his queen on f7 guards g7. [%cal Rf7g7] } 3... Qxg7 { Lost for you: a queen for a knight is a terrible trade. [%cal Gg6h6] }) 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf5 5. Qxf6 Nxg3 6. hxg3 Qxf6 7. Nxf6+ Kf7 8. Nxh5 { Queens off and three pawns up: your queen and knight got in through h6 and f6, dark squares no pawn of his can guard. That is the weak colour. [%csl Gh6,Gf6] } 1-0


