{ Berlin, 1910: you have White, as Emanuel Lasker did, world champion for 27 years, in a match for the title. No bishops are left, and you are a pawn up. }
27... Nd7 { His knight steps back to d7, a mistake: the rook to b5 was better. The weak colour again: can you find the way in? } 28. Ng5 { Yes! The knight lands on g5, a dark square no pawn of his guards, and hits his f pawn and his h pawn. His knight left f6, where it guarded the h pawn: that was his mistake. [%cal Gg5f7,Gg5h7] } (28. Ne5 { The knight jumps to e5, a dark square too, but his knight on d7 can take it. } 28... Nxe5 29. fxe5 { About level, though you are a pawn up: the knights are gone, and rooks see light and dark squares alike. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. Nd4 { The knight hops to d4, safe and central, but the only pawn it hits there, on c6, is guarded by his king. } 28... Ra7 29. h4 Ra5 { About level: his rook swings round to a5 and hits your c pawn, and his f and h pawns were never touched. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. h4 { The h pawn steps forward, but your knight stays on f3. } 28... Re8 { About level: his rook takes the e column, and your knight never jumped in. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. e4 { The e pawn strikes at d5, but he takes it, and then his pawn hits your knight from e4. } 28... dxe4 { About level: your extra pawn is gone, and your knight has to move again. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. Kb1 { The king steps to b1: safe, but it gives him the move. } 28... Rf8 { Slightly better for you: his rook guards the f pawn from f8, and your knight is still on f3. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. Rd1 { The rook comes to the d column, but his d pawn is well guarded there. } 28... Rb5 29. Rdc1 { About level: his rook hit your c pawn from b5, and your rook had to come back to guard it. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. Rd2 { The rook swings to d2 and stares at his d pawn, which his c pawn guards. } 28... Re8 { About level: his rook swings to e8, and your rook on d2 has nothing to take. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. Rg1 { The rook comes to g1 and stares at his g pawn, but two of his pawns guard it. } 28... Re8 { About level: your rook stares at a well-guarded pawn, and his rook comes to e8. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. f5 { The f pawn strikes at his g pawn, but he simply takes it. } 28... gxf5 { Level: his g pawn took yours, so your extra pawn is gone. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (28. b3 { The b pawn steps up to open lines, but his a pawn takes it with check. } 28... axb3+ 29. Ka1 bxc2 30. Rxc2 { Lost for you: his pawn took on b3 with check and then took your rook on c2. [%cal Gf3g5] }) 28... Rf8 { His rook comes to f8 to guard the f pawn. } 29. Nxh7 { Nothing guards the h pawn any more, so the knight eats it: two pawns up. } (29. Nxf7 { The knight grabs the f pawn, but his rook on f8 guards it. [%cal Rf8f7] } 29... Rxf7 { Much better for him: his rook took your knight, and a single pawn was your reward. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. h4 { The h pawn comes up to guard your knight, but it gives him a move. } 29... h6 30. Nf3 { About level: his h pawn stepped to h6 and chased your knight away, so the pawn you were after is safe. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. Rg1 { The rook comes to g1 behind your knight, but it gives him time. } 29... h6 30. Nf3 { About level: the knight was chased back to f3 before it could eat anything. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. Rd1 { The rook comes to the d column, but that gives him time to save his h pawn. } 29... h6 30. Nf3 { About level: his h pawn stepped forward and your knight went back to f3, so the pawn is safe. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. Kb1 { The king steps quietly to b1, and the h pawn you could eat now gets a move to run. } 29... h6 30. Nf3 { About level: the h pawn slipped to h6, where your knight could not eat it, and the knight had to step back. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. Rc3 { The rook lifts to c3, but it gives him a move to save his h pawn. } 29... h6 30. Nf3 { About level: his h pawn chased your knight away, and your own e pawn blocked the rook on c3 from swinging across to help. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. Rd2 { The rook slides to d2, and his h pawn gets one move to escape. } 29... h6 30. Nf3 { About level: with the h pawn on h6, your knight had nothing to eat and went back. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. e4 { The e pawn strikes at d5, but it gives him time to kick your knight first. } 29... h6 30. Nf3 dxe4 { Level: his d pawn took your e pawn and now hits your knight, and the extra pawn is gone. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. f5 { The f pawn strikes at his g pawn, but he takes it, and your knight is still waiting on g5. } 29... gxf5 { Level: the extra pawn is gone, taken by his g pawn, and the knight has still not eaten anything. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. Ne6+ { Check, and the knight hits his rook on f8 too, but his f pawn guards e6. [%cal Rf7e6] } 29... fxe6 { Much better for him: his f pawn took the knight, and the check won nothing. [%cal Gg5h7] }) (29. b3 { The b pawn steps up, but his a pawn takes it with check and marches on. } 29... axb3+ 30. Kb2 bxc2+ 31. Kxc2 { Lost for you: one pawn push, and his pawn ate your rook on the way in. [%cal Gg5h7] }) 29... Re8 { His rook jumps away from your knight, to e8. } 30. Ng5 { Back to g5, a dark square, before his f pawn reaches f6 and shuts your knight in the corner. Two pawns up: that is the weak colour, dark squares that none of his pawns guards. [%cal Gh7g5] } (30. Rg1 { The rook comes to g1 and hits the g pawn, so his f pawn cannot step forward without dropping it. Strong, but it gives his rook time to hit your c pawn. } 30... Rb5 31. Ng5 Rxc5 32. Rxc5 Nxc5 33. Nxf7 { Better for you, two pawns up again: he took your c pawn, a pair of rooks came off, and your knight took the f pawn. The knight straight back to g5 was simpler. [%cal Gh7g5] }) (30. h4 { The h pawn comes up to guard g5 first, but that is one move too slow. [%cal Gh4g5] } 30... f6 { Level, though you are two pawns up: his f pawn on f6 shuts your knight in on h7, and it has no safe square left. [%cal Gh7g5] }) (30. f5 { The f pawn strikes at his g pawn, but he takes it, and one of your two extra pawns is gone. } 30... gxf5 31. Ng5 { About level: your knight got out after all, but only one extra pawn is left. [%cal Gh7g5] }) (30. e4 { The e pawn strikes at d5, but his rook simply takes your pawn on e4. } 30... Rxe4 { About level: his rook took your e pawn, one extra pawn is gone, and your knight still sits on h7. [%cal Gh7g5] }) (30. Rd1 { The rook comes to the d column, but it gives his f pawn time to shut your knight in. } 30... f6 { About level: two pawns up on paper, but with his pawn on f6 your knight has no way out. [%cal Gh7g5] }) (30. Rd2 { The rook slides to d2, and his f pawn gets time to step to f6. } 30... f6 { Slightly better for him: your knight on h7 has no safe square, so your two extra pawns count for little. [%cal Gh7g5] }) (30. b3 { Pushing the b pawn now lets his a pawn take it with check. } 30... axb3+ 31. Ka1 bxc2 32. Rxc2 { Lost for you: his pawn swept in with check and took your rook, and you only get the pawn back. [%cal Gh7g5] }) 1-0


