{ You have Black. Your pawn has just taken on f4. }
29. exf4 { He takes back on f4, and that was a mistake: his queen to b6 was better. Can you punish it? } 29... Qd3 { Your queen lands on d3, a light square in his camp: no pawn of his can ever chase her there, and his light bishop is long gone. That is the weak colour. [%csl Gd3] } (29... Re8 { Your rook takes the open e column first, but your queen stays far away on a6. } 30. Rb8 { Level: his rook runs to b8 and hits yours along the eighth row, and your queen never got into his camp. [%cal Rb8e8,Ga6d3] }) (29... h6 { A breathing hole for your king: safe, but it gives him a free move. } 30. h4 { Level: he spends the move on his h pawn, and your queen still waits on a6. [%cal Ga6d3] }) (29... Qe2 { The queen dives to e2, a light square too, but there she only hits pieces his king guards. } 30. Qd2 Qe6 { About level: his queen offered a trade on d2, and yours had to run back to e6. [%cal Ga6d3] }) (29... Qc4 { Offering a queen trade ends your queen's trip into his camp before it starts. } 30. Qxc4 dxc4 { Better for him: the queens are gone, and his rook on b7 is now the busiest piece on the board. [%cal Ga6d3] }) (29... Bxc5 { The bishop grabs the c pawn, but his d pawn guards it. [%cal Rd4c5] } 30. dxc5 { Much better for him: a whole bishop for one pawn. [%cal Ga6d3] }) (29... h5 { The h pawn goes to h5, which does nothing for your queen and gives him a free move. } 30. Qb1 { Level: his queen used the move to reach b1, and from there she guards d3, the light square your queen wanted. [%cal Rb1d3,Ga6d3] }) (29... f5 { The f pawn steps to f5, a quiet move, and he gets time to bring his knight out. } 30. Nd2 { Level: his knight comes out to d2, and your queen has still not moved. [%cal Ga6d3] }) (29... g5 { The g pawn strikes at his f pawn, but it clears the seventh row for his rook. } 30. Qb1 { Much better for him: his queen on b1 and his rook on b7 both hit your h pawn, and only your king guards it. [%cal Rb1h7,Rb7h7] }) (29... Rb8 { Your rook comes to b8 to fight for the open b column, but nothing of yours guards b8. [%csl Rb8] } 30. Rxb8 { Lost for you: his rook took yours, and nothing can take back. [%cal Ga6d3] }) (29... Qxb7 { Your queen grabs his rook, but his queen guards it from b4. [%cal Rb4b7] } 30. Qxb7 { Lost for you: a queen for a rook, because his queen took yours straight back. [%cal Ga6d3] }) (29... Qxf1+ { Check, and your queen takes his knight, but his king takes her back. } 30. Kxf1 { Lost for you: your queen is gone, and a knight was your only reward. [%cal Ga6d3] }) 30. Kf2 { His king steps up to f2 to guard his knight and the squares around it. } 30... Re8 { Your rook takes the open e column and aims at e2, a light square right next to his king. [%cal Ge8e2] } (30... h6 { A breathing hole for your king first: slow, and it gives him time to bring his queen back. } 31. Qd2 Qxa3 32. Ne3 { Level: you still win the a3 pawn, but his knight is out on e3 and your rook still sits in the corner. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... Qxa3 { The pawn looks free, but his queen on b4 still guards it. [%cal Rb4a3] } 31. Qxa3 { Lost for you: his queen took yours, for one pawn. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... Rd8 { The rook comes to d8, but the d column is closed, so it does nothing there. } 31. Qd2 Qxa3 32. Ne3 { Level: the a3 pawn still falls, but your rook sits idle behind your own d pawn while his knight comes to e3. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... Kh8 { The king steps into the corner, a quiet move that lets his rook get busy. } 31. Rb6 Rc8 { Level: his rook hits your c pawn from b6, and your rook has to guard it from c8. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... f5 { The f pawn steps to f5, and his queen gets time to come back to d2. } 31. Qd2 Qxa3 32. Ne3 { Level: you still take the a3 pawn, but his knight on e3 now hits your d and f pawns. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... Rc8 { The rook comes to c8, where the c column is closed. } 31. Qd2 Qxa3 32. Ne3 { Level: you still grab the a3 pawn, but the rook trip to c8 was wasted and his knight jumps out. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... g5 { The g pawn strikes at his f pawn, but it gives his queen time to come back. } 31. Qd2 Qf5 32. Ng3 { Level: his queen chased yours off d3, and his knight came out to g3. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... Qc2+ { A check from c2, but his queen can block it on d2. } 31. Qd2 Qg6 32. Rb6 { About level: the check let his queen come to d2, yours had to run to g6, and his rook now hits your c pawn. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... Bxc5 { The bishop grabs the c pawn, but his queen and his d pawn both guard it. [%cal Rb4c5,Rd4c5] } 31. dxc5 { Much better for him: his d pawn took back, and your bishop is gone for one pawn. [%cal Ga8e8] }) (30... Rb8 { The rook steps to b8, where nothing of yours can take back when his rook takes. } 31. Rxb8 { Lost for you: a whole rook gone, for nothing. [%cal Ga8e8] }) 31. Qd2 { His queen runs back to d2 to stop your rook landing on e2 with check. } 31... Qxa3 { A pawn up! His queen had to run back to guard his king, and the a3 pawn lost its only guard. Your queen got in on d3, a light square no pawn of his can guard: that is the weak colour. [%csl Gd3] } (31... Qxd2+ { Trading queens with check looks tidy, but your queen is the piece that uses the light squares. } 32. Nxd2 { Better for him: with the queens gone, his rook on the seventh row does all the work, and the a3 pawn is safe. [%cal Gd3a3] }) (31... Qf5 { The queen steps back to f5 and gives up her post on d3. } 32. Ng3 { Level: the a3 pawn is safe now, and his knight is out on g3. [%cal Gd3a3] }) (31... Qg6 { The queen slides away to g6, far from the pawn on a3. } 32. h3 { Level: the a3 pawn is left alone, and he makes a breathing hole for his king. [%cal Gd3a3] }) (31... Qa6 { The queen runs back to a6, far from the light squares in his camp. } 32. Rb6 Qc8 33. Ng3 { Level: his rook chased your queen all the way back to c8, and the a3 pawn is safe. [%cal Gd3a3] }) (31... Qe4 { The queen steps to e4, a light square, but his knight can chase her from there. } 32. Ng3 Qg6 33. h3 { About level: his knight on g3 chased your queen away, and the a3 pawn is still his. [%cal Gd3a3] }) (31... Re2+ { Check with the rook from e2, but his queen simply takes it. } 32. Qxe2 Qxd4+ 33. Qe3 { Lost for you: you won a pawn on d4, but his queen took a whole rook. [%cal Gd3a3] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, his pawn wants e4"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/X0Re0OH8"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Lichess study Carlsbad Formation (Chess Structures, a Grandmaster Guide), ch. 3; Getz - Agdestein, Fagernes 2013"]
[FEN "r1bqr1k1/pp1n1ppp/2pb1n2/3p4/3P4/2NBPN2/PPQB1PPP/R3K2R w KQ - 2 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqr1k1/pp1n1ppp/2pb1n2/3p4/3P4/2NBPN2/PPQB1PPP/R3K2R_w_KQ_-_2_10"]

{ You have Black. Prophylaxis: before you start your own plan, ask what he wants to do, and stop it first. Nimzowitsch, the master who gave it that name in his book My System, ranked it above attack and defence. }
10. O-O { He castles. Now, what does he want? His e pawn to step up to e4 and let out the bishop it shuts in. Stop it first: queen to e7. [%cal Yd8e7,Re3e4][%csl Rd2] } 10... Qe7 { Your queen, with the rook behind her, your knight and your d pawn all hit e4 now. If his pawn goes there, he loses material. Now watch. [%cal Ge8e4,Gf6e4,Gd5e4][%tail] } (10... Nf8 { The knight heads for g6, a fine square, but nothing new watches e4, and his pawn can go there now. [%csl Re4] } 11. e4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 { Level: his e pawn got forward and swapped itself off, and his bishop on d2 breathes again. [%cal Gd8e7] }) (10... h6 { A handy pawn step, but it does not guard e4, the square his pawn wants. [%csl Re4] } 11. Rfe1 Nf8 12. e4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Bxe4 { Level: his rook stood behind the pawn, so the push came safely, and his pieces are free to move. [%cal Gd8e7] }) (10... Ne4 { The knight jumps to e4 before your queen can back it up, and he swaps it off at once. } 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Bxe4 Nf6 13. Bd3 { Slightly better for him: the pawn that took back on e4 fell, so you are a pawn down. The queen had to come to e7 first. [%cal Gd8e7] }) (10... b6 { A slow move on the queen side, and his pawn is free to reach e4 right away. } 11. e4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 Qc7 13. Rae1 h6 14. Nxd6 Rxe1 15. Rxe1 Qxd6 { Slightly better for him: his knight traded itself for your dark bishop, and his rook now owns the e column. [%cal Gd8e7] }) (10... a6 { A quiet pawn step on the queen side, and the square his pawn wants is still free. [%csl Re4] } 11. Rfe1 Nf8 12. e4 dxe4 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 { Level: his push to e4 came after all, and his bishop on e4 now eyes your h pawn. [%cal Gd8e7,Re4h7] }) (10... Qc7 { The queen lines up behind your bishop, aiming at h2, but she leaves e4 to his pawn. [%cal Gc7h2] } 11. Rfe1 Nf8 12. e4 dxe4 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 { Level: he got his push in and swapped it off, and your queen and bishop aim at an h pawn that his king and knight guard. [%cal Gd8e7] }) (10... Qb6 { The queen goes to b6 to eye his b pawn, and his e pawn is still free to step up. [%cal Gb6b2] } 11. Rfe1 Nf8 12. e4 dxe4 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 { Level: his e pawn went forward and traded, while his queen still guards the b pawn you were eyeing. [%cal Gd8e7] }) (10... Nb6 { The knight heads for c4, a nice square, but it does nothing about e4. } 11. Rfe1 h6 12. h3 Be6 13. a3 Rc8 14. e4 { Level: after some quiet moves his pawns stand side by side on d4 and e4, and the push he wanted is made. [%cal Gd8e7] }) 11. h3 Ne4 12. Be1 Ndf6 { Your knight sits on e4, backed by the other knight and your d pawn, so his e pawn is stuck, and his dark bishop still has no open diagonal. About level, but his plan is gone: that is prophylaxis. [%cal Gf6e4,Gd5e4][%csl Re3,Re1] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, make his push cost him"]
