{ You have White. The pawns in the middle are jammed together, and both sides are regrouping. }
17... Na5 { His knight goes to the edge. It looks harmless, but it is a slip. } 18. f3 { Right: your f pawn now guards e4, so his knight on f6 cannot land there safely. The jump there at once was his better try. Botvinnik, a future world champion, found this against Capablanca, a former one. [%cal Gf3e4,Rf6e4] } (18. Re2 { The rook steps up to e2, but e4 is still open for his knight. } 18... Ne4 19. Nxe4 dxe4 { Level: his knight landed on e4, and after the swap his d pawn stands there, blocking your e pawn. [%cal Gf2f3] }) (18. e4 { Your e pawn goes forward at once, before it has enough guards. } 18... dxe4 19. Re2 Nb3 20. Rfe1 h6 { Slightly better for him: the push came too soon, the pawn fell, and you are a pawn down. [%cal Gf2f3] }) (18. Bc1 { The bishop drops back to c1, and e4 is still free for his knight. } 18... Ne4 { About level: his knight sits on e4 already, planted in front of your e pawn. [%cal Gf2f3] }) (18. h3 { A small pawn step for your king, and it gives his rooks time to gang up on your e pawn. } 18... Re6 19. f3 Qc7 20. Qf2 Rae8 { Level: your f pawn came a move late, and his rooks now stand doubled on the e column. [%cal Re6e3] }) (18. Qf5 { Your queen offers a trade on f5, and still no pawn of yours guards e4. [%csl Re4] } 18... Qxf5 19. Nxf5 g6 20. Ng3 { Level: the queens are off, your knight is back on g3, and his knight can still use e4. [%cal Gf2f3] }) (18. Rb1 { Your rook heads for the b column, and e4 stays open for his knight. } 18... Nb3 19. Rfe1 Ne4 { Slightly better for him: his knight sits on e4, and his queen now hits your a4 pawn. [%cal Gf2f3,Rd7a4] }) (18. Ra1 { The rook steps over to guard your a pawn, but e4 is still open for his knight. } 18... Nb3 19. Ra2 Re6 { Slightly better for him: your rook guards the a pawn from a2, but his knight can still jump to e4. [%cal Gf2f3] }) (18. Qxh7+ { The queen grabs h7 with check, but his knight simply takes her. } 18... Nxh7 { Lost for you: your queen is gone for one pawn, and nothing follows. [%cal Gf2f3] }) 18... Nb3 { His knight lands on b3 and cuts your queen off from the a4 pawn, which his queen now hits. [%cal Rd7a4] } 19. e4 { That is it: his plan is stopped, so start yours. Your e pawn steps up to e4, guarded by the f pawn. The a4 pawn can wait: grabbing it would let your pawns roll on. Now watch. [%cal Gf3e4][%tail] } (19. Re2 { The rook backs up your e pawn, but meanwhile his queen can take the a4 pawn. } 19... Qxa4 20. e4 { About level: your push came in the end, but his queen took the a4 pawn first. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (19. Qf2 { The queen steps over to f2 first, so your e pawn has to wait a move. } 19... Kh8 20. e4 Ng8 { Slightly better for you: the push came a move late, and the a4 pawn is still loose. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (19. a5 { The a pawn runs from his queen, straight into the reach of his knight. } 19... Nxa5 20. e4 Kh8 { Slightly better for you: your e pawn went forward in the end, but the a pawn went for nothing. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (19. Qd1 { The queen drops back to d1, and the push you wanted waits another move. } 19... Kh8 20. e4 a5 { About level: your push came late, and his a pawn is now on the move on the queen side. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (19. Rf2 { The rook steps up to f2 to guard your second row, but the a4 pawn is left for his queen. } 19... Qxa4 20. e4 Kh8 { About level: the push arrived a move late and a pawn short, because his queen had time to grab on a4. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (19. Rb1 { Your rook leaves for the b column, and the pawn on e3 loses its guard. } 19... Rxe3 20. Bc1 Nxc1 21. Qxc1 { Better for him: his rook took your e3 pawn, so there is no e pawn left to push. [%csl Re3] }) (19. Ra1 { The rook comes to guard the a4 pawn, but a1 is a square his knight hits. [%cal Ga1a4,Rb3a1] } 19... Nxa1 20. Rxa1 { Much better for him: his knight took your rook, and you only got the knight back. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (19. Qxb3 { Your queen grabs his knight, but his c pawn takes her back. } 19... cxb3 { Lost for you: your queen went for a single knight. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (19. Qxh7+ { The queen crashes into h7 with check, and his knight takes her. } 19... Nxh7 { Lost for you: a queen sacrifice with nothing behind it. [%cal Ge3e4] }) 19... Kh8 20. e5 Ng8 21. Qf5 { His knight never reached e4 and is back on g8, where it began the game, while your pawns hold the middle. First his plan, then yours: that is prophylaxis. [%cal Gf3e4,Ge5f6][%csl Rg8] } 1-0
